(Q_load = S_load * sin\(Phi\) = 12 * 0.60 = 7.20 MVAR = 7,200 kVAR) Tj
T*
(- Initial estimated line current: I_L = \(12 * 10^6\) / \(sqrt\(3\) * 33,000\) = 209.95 A.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Voltage Drop and Voltage Regulation Calculation:) Tj
T*
(- Approximate phase voltage drop formula: Delta_V_ph = I_L * \(R * cos\(Phi\) + X *) Tj
T*
(sin\(Phi\)\)) Tj
T*
(- Delta_V_ph = 209.95 * [ \(2.56 * 0.80\) + \(4.64 * 0.60\) ] = 209.95 * [ 2.048 + 2.784 ] =) Tj
T*
(209.95 * 4.832 = 1,014.5 V per phase.) Tj
T*
(- Line-to-line voltage drop: Delta_V_LL = sqrt\(3\) * 1,014.5 = 1,757.2 V = 1.76 kV.) Tj
T*
(- Receiving End Voltage: V_r = V_s - Delta_V_LL = 33.0 - 1.76 = 31.24 kV.) Tj
T*
(- Percentage Voltage Regulation = [ \(V_s - V_r\) / V_r ] * 100 = \(1.76 / 31.24\) * 100 =) Tj
T*
(5.63%.) Tj
T*
(- Total 3-Phase Line Loss: P_loss = 3 * I_L^2 * R_line = 3 * \(209.95\)^2 * 2.56 = 3 *) Tj
T*
(44,079 * 2.56 = 338,527 W = 338.53 kW.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Shunt Capacitor Bank Sizing for Power Factor Correction to 0.96 Lagging:) Tj
T*
(- Desired power factor: cos\(Phi_new\) = 0.96 => tan\(Phi_new\) = tan\(acos\(0.96\)\) =) Tj
T*
(tan\(16.26 deg\) = 0.2917.) Tj
T*
(- Initial power factor angle: cos\(Phi_old\) = 0.80 => tan\(Phi_old\) = 0.7500.) Tj
T*
(- Required reactive power compensation: Q_cap = P_load * [ tan\(Phi_old\) - tan\(Phi_new\) ]) Tj
T*
(- Q_cap = 9.60 MW * [ 0.7500 - 0.2917 ] = 9.60 * 0.4583 = 4.40 MVAR.) Tj
T*
(- Practical Specification: Install a 4.5 MVAR, 33 kV automatic switched capacitor bank) Tj
T*
(\(three 1.5 MVAR stages\).) Tj
T*
(- Post-compensation line current drops to: I_new = \(9.60 * 10^6\) / \(sqrt\(3\) * 33,000 *) Tj
T*
(0.96\) = 174.96 A.) Tj
T*
(- Reduced line losses: P_loss_new = 3 * \(174.96\)^2 * 2.56 = 235,093 W = 235.09 kW) Tj
T*
(\(Savings of 103.4 kW!\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> Example 3.2: Digital PI Controller and Filter Sizing for Synchronous Buck Converter) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Statement: An electrical engineer is designing a high-efficiency DC-DC synchronous buck) Tj
T*
(converter to power an industrial IoT controller board from a 48 V telecom battery backup) Tj
T*
(bus. The design requirements are:) Tj
T*
(- Input DC Voltage: V_in = 48.0 V \(+/- 10%\)) Tj
T*
(- Regulated Output DC Voltage: V_out = 12.0 V) Tj
T*
(- Rated Load Current: I_out = 15.0 A) Tj
T*
(- Switching Frequency: f_sw = 100 kHz \(T_sw = 10 microseconds\)) Tj
T*
(- Maximum Allowed Inductor Current Ripple: Delta_I_L <= 20% of rated I_out) Tj
T*
(- Maximum Allowed Output Voltage Ripple: Delta_V_out <= 1.0% of nominal V_out \(120 mV\)) Tj
T*
(\(a\) Calculate the steady-state duty cycle \(D\).) Tj
T*
(\(b\) Calculate the minimum filter inductance \(L\) and output filter capacitance \(C\).) Tj
T*
(\(c\) Determine the digital sampling frequency and discrete PI controller transfer) Tj
T*
(function parameters using Bilinear Tustin Transformation for continuous gains K_p = 1.8) Tj
T*
(and K_i = 850.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

